<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lausanne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lausanne is a city on the northern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-geneva">Lake Geneva</a>, the capital of the Swiss canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a>. It was the seat of a bishopric from about 600, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> ruled the town as princes until Bern conquered Vaud in 1536 and introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. Its Gothic cathedral was consecrated in 1275, and the city is now home to the Federal Supreme Court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and the International Olympic Committee.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lausanne.jpg" alt="Lausanne" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arnaud Gaillard (arnaud amarys.com); Gzzz; Roland Zumbühl. Original uploader was Hagelhans at de.wikipedia; Rama; Composition: Noldo18 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lausanne_Wiki.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…his position by granting counties to loyal bishops, giving the county of Valais to the bishop of Sion in 999 and the county of Vaud to the bishop of <b>Lausanne</b> in 1011, and in agreements from 1006 onwards he recognised his nephew, Emperor Henry II, as his heir.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Its people were the product of a century of flight: five to nine million Muslim refugees had come into Anatolia from the Balkans, the Caucasus, Crimea and the Mediterranean islands as the empire contracted, and the <b>Lausanne</b> Convention added a population exchange with Greece.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The total revision of 1874 added the optional referendum on federal laws and strengthened the Federal Supreme Court in <b>Lausanne</b>, and the popular initiative for constitutional amendments followed in 1891.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The bronzes were first exhibited in Beijing in 1987 and 1990 and at the Olympic Museum in <b>Lausanne</b> in 1993.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sanxingdui-culture">Sanxingdui culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The kings controlled the Great St Bernard pass and relied on rich abbeys such as Saint-Maurice and on alliances with local bishops at <b>Lausanne</b>, Geneva and Sion.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-upper-burgundy">Kingdom of Upper Burgundy</a></li><li><blockquote>The bishops of the Avenches area moved their seat to <b>Lausanne</b> around 600.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-lausanne">Prince-Bishopric of Lausanne</a></li></ul></section>
